      "content": "I understand, though I'd prefer if we could keep this strictly professional and avoid the storytelling approach. I'm looking for a more technical breakdown. \n\nCould you specifically address how Singaporean courts interpret the threshold for \"unforeseeability\" in these clauses? For instance, if a tenant is dealing with a sudden government-mandated lockdown, how does the court determine if that specific disruption qualifies as an unforeseeable force majeure event under a standard retail lease?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under Singaporean law, the interpretation of \"force majeure\" clauses, particularly concerning the threshold for \"unforeseeability,\" is a nuanced process that hinges on the specific language of the contract and the circumstances surrounding the event in question. When assessing whether a government-mandated lockdown qualifies as an unforeseeable force majeure event under a standard retail lease, courts will typically consider several factors.\n\nFirstly, the courts will examine the precise wording of the force majeure clause within the lease agreement. The clause should explicitly list the types of events that are considered force majeure, and the language used will be critical in determining whether a government-mandated lockdown falls within this scope. If the clause is broad and includes terms like \"government actions\" or \"regulatory changes,\" there is a stronger argument that such an event could be deemed force majeure.\n\nSecondly, the courts will assess the foreseeability of the event at the time the lease was executed. This involves evaluating whether the parties could have reasonably anticipated the lockdown when they entered into the lease. If the lockdown was a result of a sudden and unexpected outbreak of a pandemic, which was not foreseeable at the time of the lease agreement, the event may be considered unforeseeable.\n\nThirdly, the courts will consider",
