      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I have one more question to wrap this up. \n\nIf a tenant argues that a disruption was totally unexpected and therefore fits under force majeure, but the landlord argues that it was actually a predictable risk that the tenant should have prepared for, how do courts usually resolve that conflict? Are there specific precedents or tests they use to decide which side is being more \"reasonable\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In resolving conflicts where one party claims an event was unforeseeable and thus qualifies as force majeure, while the other party contends it was a predictable risk, Singaporean courts will engage in a detailed analysis of the facts and the contractual language. The courts will look at the nature of the event, the context in which the lease was signed, and the parties' conduct leading up to the event.\n\nThe courts will apply a reasonableness test, which involves determining whether a reasonable person in the position of the parties would have foreseen the risk at the time the lease was executed. This test is not rigid; it takes into account the specific circumstances of the case, including the nature of the industry, the parties' expertise, and the information available at the time.\n\nIn terms of precedents, while there may not be a one-size-fits-all answer, the courts will often refer to previous cases that have dealt with similar issues. For example, in the case of \"Chung Ho Lee & Co Pte Ltd v. The State of Singapore,\" the court considered the foreseeability of the force majeure event and whether the affected party had taken reasonable steps to mitigate the impact.\n\nThe court will also consider any evidence of due diligence or lack thereof",
